{
  "about": "Shared test vectors for the SeeForMe voice-command parser. Python (seeforme/live/voice.py) and the web app (web/src/voice.ts) must both pass every case. 'wake' cases are hands-free: the wake phrase ('see for me' and its mis-hearings) is required and the command is the text after the last wake phrase. 'direct' cases are push-to-talk or typed: the wake phrase is optional. intent null means: ignore the utterance. Arguments are lowercase. A find target has no leading 'me' ('find me a chair'), no 'the way to', articles or 'nearest'/'closest', and spoken numbers as digits; 'the way out' and 'the way out of here' are 'exit'. Openings 'please' and 'can you' are dropped; 'tell me', 'do you know' and 'find out' (and a 'please' after them) only before a question word, 'the way to' or 'the way out' ('tell me goodbye' is a question). The weak FIND verbs ('get me', 'get me to', 'how do I get to / find / reach', 'the way to', and 'show me') take a target only when a noun phrase follows: a determiner first ('the exit', 'a chair', 'my keys'), a name with a number ('room 204'), a known target without a determiner (the detector's labels and the travel places of voice.py known_targets: 'how do I get to elevator', since recognisers drop a short 'the') or 'out of here' / 'the way out'; otherwise the text stays a question ('get me started', 'how do I get to sleep', 'the way to the exit is blocked'). pause has arg 'camera' when the phrase names the camera ('camera off', 'stop the camera'; the terminal demo then says that its camera stays on) and null otherwise ('pause', 'stop talking'). flip has arg 'front' or 'back' when the phrase names a camera side ('front camera', 'selfie camera' -> front; 'back camera', 'rear camera', 'switch to the back camera' -> back) and null when it does not ('flip camera', 'switch camera', 'other camera'). New phrasings go to 'direct' (the wake phrase is optional there, so 'see for me ...' forms fit too) or 'unicode', never to 'wake'. 'unicode' cases (mode 'wake' or 'direct' each) hold non-ASCII text: both parsers use Unicode words (Python's \\w: letters, numbers, '_'), word boundaries and decimal digits, and NFC first. They are kept apart from 'wake' because scripts/eval_voice.py synthesises the 'wake' cases. 'refine' cases: may the lower reading 'alt' of the same speech (a lower Web Speech alternative; in the terminal, the command grammar's re-decode) replace the reading 'best', in mode 'wake' or 'direct' (web mayRefine, Python may_refine)? Only when best is the bare wake phrase or free text and alt is a command; after a question only a command that answers or looks for something (find, describe, read, help, repeat); after other free text not a command that changes something and is best with words left out ('tell me goodbye' is not 'goodbye'), the words compared without polite openings and trailing 'please' / 'now' ('goodbye please' and 'so goodbye' leave words of 'tell me goodbye' out too).",
